# Canonical workflow adapter

This file adapts Cursor behavior to the repository workflow. It does not own or
redefine task, evidence, Git, blocker, confirmation, performance, or closure
semantics. The canonical workflow is
[`DEVELOPMENT-OPERATING-MODEL.md`](../../docs/governance/DEVELOPMENT-OPERATING-MODEL.md),
including:

- `TASK-ATOMIC-DELIVERY-01`;
- `CHECKPOINT-DELIVERY-01`;
- `CONTINUOUS-AUTONOMOUS-DELIVERY-01`;
- `CAMPAIGN-BACKLOG-CONTINUATION-01`;
- `RESOLVE-BEFORE-BLOCKED-PROGRESS-01`;
- required interactive confirmation boundaries;
- the performance validation ladder; and
- deterministic task closure.

If this adapter conflicts with a canonical repository source, follow the
canonical source and correct this adapter in the same scoped delivery.

# Campaign objective

When the owner activates continuous delivery (this rule plus a standing session
prompt), the default objective is to drive `PROGRESS.md` Layer 1 Remaining to
`0` for all formal Personal tasks, subject only to axioms, confirmation
boundaries, and genuine external blockers. Do not treat one task merge, one
Slice close, one PR, or one Gate ask as the end of the campaign.

# Continuation and recovery

Treat `继续任务`, `继续`, `continue task`, an equivalent request, a new window,
or context compression as a continuation event. Recover from repository facts
instead of asking the user to restate history:

1. read `AGENTS.md`, project identity, and the Operating Model;
2. read the Personal formal plan for task acceptance and Slice definitions;
3. read only `PROGRESS.md` Current snapshot for current facts;
4. read the active lease table before selecting writable paths;
5. read only the latest matching handoff and relevant task card;
6. verify branch, worktree, HEAD/upstream, Draft PR, and required checks.

Never read or use `History/` as an operational source. Chat, branch names, old
prompts, and historical handoffs cannot override canonical repository facts.

# Cursor execution behavior

- Continue the active formal task and its unique `in-progress` Slice. If no
  task is active, select one implementation-ready Personal task and claim one
  narrow task lease before editing.
- After a task is fully closed (ready/merge/lease/branch/main), **in the same
  turn** claim the next ready task and start its first vertical implementation
  slice. Do not end the turn after claim/docs-only registration.
- Use one task branch, one evolving Draft PR, and one task lease through full
  acceptance. Do not create Slice branches, Slice PRs, routine Slice handoffs,
  or separate acceptance-assessment work.
- Prefer a real caller, durable authority outcome, or end-to-end correctness
  property over consecutive helper-only changes.
- Use focused failure-first or negative tests when they materially reduce
  regression risk. Do not add tests that merely restate implementation.
- For Gate/campaign slices, prefer an ADR-0040-class fixed authority-path
  denominator and non-claim report over live Provider/Pi statistical campaigns
  unless the formal acceptance explicitly forbids the MVP path.
- Protect unknown or user-owned changes. Stop for user direction when their
  ownership cannot be resolved; never overwrite, revert, stage, or mix them.
- Respect daemon-only authority, persist-before-dispatch Intent/Effect,
  fencing, budget, independent verification, secret isolation, and all
  registered safety boundaries.
- Route Rust/runtime validation according to
  `PERSONAL-TEST-ENVIRONMENTS.md`. Remote Linux consumes only pushed exact Git
  revisions; Windows GNU does not rerun known unsupported Rust linking.
- Keep campaign guests isolated from ordinary development.
- Under standing continuous-delivery authorization, coherent secret-free
  checkpoint commit/push and Draft PR maintenance are expected workflow events,
  not per-turn permission asks.

# Forbidden stop conditions

Do **not** end a continuous-delivery turn solely because of any of the
following:

- a Slice, commit, push, CI start/finish, or Draft PR update completed;
- a task was claimed or Delivery Slices were registered without code progress;
- a mid-flight status summary was written to chat;
- context feels long (persist next action into `PROGRESS.md` and continue);
- Gate disposition was prepared for the owner (record `blocked` + recovery
  action, then claim a non-overlapping ready task and continue);
- local Windows GNU cannot run Rust (route to CI/Linux and continue eligible
  work).

Valid stop conditions are only: owner pause/scope change; required interactive
confirmation boundary with alternatives exhausted; unknown concurrent changes;
or no remaining ready tasks (Remaining = 0, or only blocked items awaiting
owner/external recovery).

# Persistence, blockers, and reporting

Checkpoint only when remote CI, exact-revision validation, or abnormal recovery
needs an immutable revision. A checkpoint is a background persistence event,
not a Slice closure, report boundary, handoff trigger, ready/merge decision, or
reason to stop. Keep the PR Draft until complete task acceptance is satisfied.

Repair task-owned code, tests, formatting, CI configuration, integration, and
recoverable environment failures directly. Record a formal blocker and ask for
interaction only at the Operating Model's owner-only confirmation boundaries
or after all supported recovery routes are exhausted. Never bypass a required
failure or merge an incomplete task.

Update canonical documents with durable facts, not chat summaries. Create one
handoff only for complete task closure, explicit pause/transfer, unknown
changes, or a genuine external blocker. When the whole task is accepted, run
the deterministic ready/merge/lease/branch/main reconciliation sequence. If no
decision or blocker exists, continue in the same response instead of ending at
an intermediate checkpoint. Before context compression or a new window, persist
the unique next action, completed/remaining items, exact revision, PR, and
lease into `PROGRESS.md` Current snapshot.
